{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01T7vNtPavDL8ZeM6hgJQAD9","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (52.5KB). Full output saved to: /ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M86VP4D6CD8WXDR7ZMA6BKSF/6daadd15-ae85-4ac6-8f77-85168753bba0/tool-results/bhlxatvlv.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n82:| `ard:needs-approval` | Merger | PR touches a protected path; merges after a write-access human approves its head with a GitHub review |\n124:| a custom triage role | its role file only, unless `config.yaml` gives it `plan_section: <heading>` (see Configuration) |\n160:**One committer for main.** Close-outs never push. They hand a file set to the controller's committer, which keeps its own clean worktree of `main`, commits as `ard[bot]` with `ard(<stage>:<role>): #<issue> <summary> [skip ci]`, re-applies its snapshot on the fetched head, pushes with a lease, and retries up to 3 times (re-applying the whole-file snapshot is the rebase; if upstream changed one of its paths the request fails with a conflict instead of overwriting). A replay of an unchanged tree is a no-op that returns the upstream head. It refuses any change outside the `.ard/<issue#>/` directories. Worker code takes a separate lane: `Branches.CommitCode` commits only paths `committer.CheckCodePath` accepts (nothing under `.ard/`, `.ard-input/` or `.github/`, no `.git` or secret-shaped names), only to `worker/` branches, creating a branch with a push whose lease expects it to be absent and refusing to overwrite a path that changed on the branch since the job started. The one other lane is `ard init`'s: it commits only `.ard/config.yaml` and files under the four role trees, only to the new branch `ard-init` cut from `main`, with a push whose lease expects the ref to be absent, so it creates the branch and never updates an existing one, and opens a PR; a human merges it with a squash or merge commit, so the bot-commit check never sees a bot commit outside `.ard/<issue#>/` on main's first-parent history. Because the bot must bypass branch protection on `main`, a detective check audits it: the workflow `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` walks the first-parent history of `main` (the last 500 commits, or everything after the repository variable `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`) and fails the run, listing the commits\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8328e4e2-1211-4885-a6f6-74fec4bedb87","uuid":"f7f1f413-828c-40b0-baec-34cf028c89c7","timestamp":"2026-11-24T13:25:01.157Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"82:| `ard:needs-approval` | Merger | PR touches a protected path; merges after a write-access human approves its head with a GitHub review |\n124:| a custom triage role | its role file only, unless `config.yaml` gives it `plan_section: <heading>` (see Configuration) |\n160:**One committer for main.** Close-outs never push. They hand a file set to the controller's committer, which keeps its own clean worktree of `main`, commits as `ard[bot]` with `ard(<stage>:<role>): #<issue> <summary> [skip ci]`, re-applies its snapshot on the fetched head, pushes with a lease, and retries up to 3 times (re-applying the whole-file snapshot is the rebase; if upstream changed one of its paths the request fails with a conflict instead of overwriting). A replay of an unchanged tree is a no-op that returns the upstream head. It refuses any change outside the `.ard/<issue#>/` directories. Worker code takes a separate lane: `Branches.CommitCode` commits only paths `committer.CheckCodePath` accepts (nothing under `.ard/`, `.ard-input/` or `.github/`, no `.git` or secret-shaped names), only to `worker/` branches, creating a branch with a push whose lease expects it to be absent and refusing to overwrite a path that changed on the branch since the job started. The one other lane is `ard init`'s: it commits only `.ard/config.yaml` and files under the four role trees, only to the new branch `ard-init` cut from `main`, with a push whose lease expects the ref to be absent, so it creates the branch and never updates an existing one, and opens a PR; a human merges it with a squash or merge commit, so the bot-commit check never sees a bot commit outside `.ard/<issue#>/` on main's first-parent history. Because the bot must bypass branch protection on `main`, a detective check audits it: the workflow `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` walks the first-parent history of `main` (the last 500 commits, or everything after the repository variable `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`) and fails the run, listing the commits and paths in the job summary, when a commit made by the bot changes anything but regular files under a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. Bot commits are identified by **committer** (the App's noreply email or login), not author, because a squash merge of a bot PR has the bot as author. If the 500-commit window is full and its oldest commit is the bot's, the check fails closed until `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE` is set, so a bad commit cannot be flushed out of view. It runs hourly as well as on push, because `[skip ci]` suppresses the push trigger. It fails closed when the `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` repository variable is unset. It reads both variables from repository settings and lives in `.github/workflows/`, which the App cannot write. Alerting is the failed run only; it does not open issues or pause ard.\n206:**The stale closer is a built-in scanner.** It has no role directory and needs no job slot, executor or worktree; its settings live in the top-level `stale:` block of `config.yaml`. It is a plain controller loop that runs every 24 hours and is skipped while paused. It closes scanner issues still at `ard:pending` with no activity (issues filed under `route: none` or `needs-human` lack `ard:pending` and are never candidates; #204 may widen this) for `stale.inactive_for` (default 30 days), as not planned, with a comment. Activity means a timeline event by anyone other than the ard App. It never closes an issue that has a `:running` label, `ard:hold`, `ard:needs-human`, a linked open PR, or a human author (any author other than the App), and it closes nothing when the client is not authenticated as an App. It only comments and closes: suppression happens on the owning scanner's next reconcile, which suppresses every not-planned close for `stale.suppress_for`. A reopened issue gets a fresh comment when it goes stale again.\n224:**The lint scanner** (`scanners/lint`, a `sh` script, one `jq` program and a Dockerfile that builds golangci-lint v2 with the image's Go) is Go only in v1; other ecosystems are follow-ups. It runs golangci-lint once per `go.mod` (pruning `.git`, `vendor`, `node_modules` and `testdata`) with the repo's own config, or a human-owned `golangci.yml` in the role directory when present. These flags are always forced after any config, because findings that vanish are autoclosed: `--max-issues-per-linter=0 --max-same-issues=0` (no truncation), `--new=false --new-from-rev= --new-from-merge-base= --new-from-patch=` (a repo config asking for \"new issues only\" would report nothing on a clean checkout), `--issues-exit-code=0 --fix=false --path-mode=abs`, plus JSON output to a temp file. It fails closed: a golangci-lint exit other than 0, a `typecheck` issue (a compile error), a module needing a newer Go than the image's (`GOTOOLCHAIN=local`; toolchain switching is a follow-up), a path outside the repository or with a control character, a malformed baseline, and a missing `baseline` file all exit non-zero with no output. Because the image builds golangci-lint with `CGO_ENABLED=0`, a repo config's `goplugin` custom linter cannot load and fails the run. The fingerprint is `lint:<rule>:<path>:<msg>`. `<rule>` is the linter plus `/<code>` for a check code (`staticcheck/SA4000`, `gosec/G104`), or for `govet` and `revive` an analyzer name (`govet/printf`). `<msg>` drops the code, control characters and repeated whitespace, and replaces every standalone number with `N`, so line numbers and \"complexity 31\" versus \"32\" never move a fingerprint; a fingerprint over 512 bytes has its message cut by runes. Identical fingerprints in one file collapse into one finding listing every line; the known gap is that a new occurrence of an identical message in the same file is hidden while the old one is open or baselined (a count-aware baseline is a follow-up). The group is `<rule>:<directory>`, so with `group_size: 20` issues are 20 per rule and directory. Severity has two levels: `medium` for `govet`, `staticcheck/SA*`, `gosec*`, `bodyclose`, `nilerr`, `nilnesserr`, `rowserrcheck`, `sqlclosecheck` and `durationcheck`, `low` for everything else; golangci-lint's own severity is ignored. Messages, source lines and paths are untrusted: they are rendered only inside `text` fences with ``` neutralised, and inline code spans have backticks replaced. Every `//nolint` directive in a `.go` file is itself a finding (rule `nolint`, group `nolint:<directory>`, `low`), so a worker cannot close a lint issue by suppressing it; for the same reason the starter protects `**/.golangci.{yml,yaml,toml,json}`. The baseline is the human-owned file `baseline` in the role directory (a protected path, so no agent can baseline a finding): `#` comments and blank lines are ignored, every other line is one fingerprint, and a finding whose fingerprint is listed is dropped. `ard init` does not ship one, so the scanner records a failure every 24 h until a human commits it; an empty file means no baseline. `scan --baseline` prints a baseline of all current findings (ignoring any existing one), and the exact Docker command is in the Dockerfile's header; the run logs how many baseline entries are unused. Baselining a finding that is open closes its issue as \"No longer detected\" (completed); to suppress it instead, close the issue as not planned. A golangci-lint version bump can change message text and so every fingerprint: bump the version and regenerate every repo's `baseline` in the same human PR.\n226:**The error-codes scanner** (`scanners/error-codes`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program, pattern match over source, registry and docs files) reads a human-owned `codes.json` in its role directory; nothing ships one, because error-code conventions are specific to each repository. Keys: `pattern` (required, a regex of at most 512 characters, wrapped so `E1001` does not match inside `XE10011`; it must not match the empty string), `registry` (required, a list of path patterns), `entry` (optional: a registry line must also match it to define a code), `docs` (optional; unset turns the undocumented check off), `sources` (optional, default every text file) and `exclude`. A path pattern ending in `/` is a directory prefix from the root, one without `/` matches the base name, and any other is a glob on the whole path (`*` and `?` stop at `/`, `**` does not). Unknown keys, wrong types and lists over 100 entries are errors. Codes found in registry files (on `entry` lines) are R, codes anywhere in docs files are D, codes in every other text file are S. Findings: `error-codes:duplicated:<code>` (high, group `duplicate codes`: a code on two or more distinct registry lines), `error-codes:unregistered:<code>` (medium, group `unregistered codes`: in S, not in R) and `error-codes:undocumented:<code>` (low, group `undocumented codes`: in R, not in D, only when `docs` is set). Fingerprints use the code alone, so moving a use never churns an issue. `.git`, `.ard`, `vendor`, `node_modules`, `testdata`, symlinks, binary files, files of about 1 MiB or more and lines over 16 KiB are skipped. A finding carries only the code and repo-relative `path:line` locations, never line text, and codes outside `[A-Za-z0-9_.:/+=-]{1,128}` are dropped with a stderr count. Because the scanner is `autoclose`, it fails the run rather than see less: an invalid `codes.json`, a `registry` or `docs` that matches no file, a file in both, an unreadable file, a failing tool or more than 5,000 findings all exit non-zero with no output. With no `codes.json` it logs `not configured` and succeeds with no findings, so deleting `codes.json` closes its open issues as \"No longer detected\". ard does not run it on itself until ard has error codes.\n228:**The missing-tests scanner** (`scanners/missing-tests`, a `sh` script plus a stdlib-only Go helper) runs each Go module's own tests with `go test -coverpkg=./...` and files one finding per exported function or method that no test calls by name or that the tests never run; the group is the package, and the fingerprint does not depend on the file or line. Because it runs the repository's tests it needs the executor's container and egress to the module proxy. If a module's tests fail to build or fail, the scan fails and files nothing, so autoclose never acts on partial coverage. A human-owned `ignore` file next to `scan` skips symbols or packages, and a `no-coverage` file there skips the test run and reports only the static rule. It ships in the starter tree but is not enabled on ard's own `.ard/`.\n230:**The lts scanner** (`scanners/lts`, a `sh` script plus two `jq` programs and `yq` for Helm, no build step) flags components at or near end of life using the endoflife.date v1 API (`/api/v1/products/<slug>`, one request per referenced product). A component is a product plus a release cycle. Its fingerprint is `eol:<product>:<cycle>` (for example `eol:nodejs:18`), one finding lists every referencing file, and `group` is the product. The human-owned `products.json` maps Docker image names and a few npm packages to endoflife.date slugs (the Alpine image maps to `alpine-linux`); names not in it are skipped with a stderr line. Cycles match generically: a codename's first word (`bookworm`, `jammy`), else the longest dotted prefix of the version. It reads the `toolchain` line (else the `go` directive) of `go.mod`; `engines.node` and table npm dependencies of `package.json` (lowest lower bound of the range); Dockerfile `FROM` lines (`Dockerfile`, `Dockerfile.*`, `*.Dockerfile`, `Containerfile`; global `ARG` substitution, registry and digest stripped); and `repository`/`tag` or `image: name:tag` in the `values.yaml` beside each `Chart.yaml`. Severity is `high` at or past end of life, `medium` within 90 days, `low` within 180 days, otherwise no finding. A component referenced only by floors (a `go` directive, `engines.node`, an npm range) is capped at `medium`. Titles carry the date (`Go 1.21: end of life 2024-08-13`), never \"approaching\" or \"past\", because rerate changes only severity. The scan fails closed with nothing on stdout, because an absent finding closes its issue: any API failure (including a 404 for a mapped product, which means `products.json` is stale), a non-JSON response, and a `package.json` or `values.yaml` that cannot be parsed. A missing tool exits 2 (`yq` only when a `Chart.yaml` exists). Paths with a `.git`, `vendor`, `node_modules` or `testdata` segment are skipped. Manifest text reaches the issue body only as cleaned text in code spans (control characters and backticks removed, 200 characters, at most 50 references), and repo text never reaches a URL.\n232:**The trufflehog scanner** (`scanners/trufflehog`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program) runs `trufflehog filesystem . --json --no-update --no-color --no-verification --no-ignore-tag --fail-on-scan-errors --concurrency=2` on the current commit. It never verifies a credential against a provider. Only the detector name, file, line and a hash of the secret leave the scan: `Raw`, `RawV2`, `Redacted`, `SecretParts`, `ExtraData` and `StructuredData` stay in pipes inside `scan`. The fingerprint is `trufflehog:` plus the first 16 hex digits of SHA-256 over `ard-trufflehog-v1`, a NUL byte and the base64 of the secret (an unsalted hash, so one value gives one issue wherever it appears). The scan fails closed: a non-zero trufflehog exit, a missing `finished scanning` summary, zero chunks on a non-empty tree, or a result count that differs from the summary produces no findings, so nothing is autoclosed on a partial run. `exclude-paths` (regexes; `.git` is always excluded) and `allow` (one fingerprint per line) are human-owned; a bad `allow` entry fails the run. Findings use `route: needs-human`, severity high, and carry a runbook that says to rotate the credential first. `commit` is the scanned SHA, recorded in `.ard/<n>/issue.md`; history is scanned by `scanners/trufflehog-history`. The forbidden flags (`--since-commit`, `--max-depth`, `--detector-timeout`, `--archive-timeout`, `--filter-unverified`, `--filter-entropy`, `--results`) are never passed, because a partial or load-dependent result would autoclose live findings. Caveats: (1) a secret gone from HEAD is not revoked; autoclose closes the issue as completed, so the issue body tells the human to rotate first. (2) The fingerprint hash is unsalted, an offline oracle for a low-entropy value that is already in the repo at HEAD; accepted. (3) Issues on a public repo show `file:line`. (4) At `max_open` the scanner is skipped, so new leaks go unseen until a human resolves one; scanner failure streaks are posted on the status issue (#221). (5) Any future change that commits scanner logs must exclude this scanner. (6) On a public repo with GitHub secret scanning off, this scanner is the only detection channel, and it cannot stop a push. TruffleHog is AGPL-3.0: it is installed by the role's Dockerfile (or a host install) and is not in the published `ard` image or the root `Dockerfile`. Host and Docker integration tests (`go test -tags integration -run Trufflehog ./internal/roles/`, with `ARD_TRUFFLEHOG_BIN` at the Dockerfile's pinned version) prove the role against a real binary and image.\n236:**The docs scanner** (`scanners/docs`, a `sh` script plus one stdlib-only Go program, `docscan.go`, built at scan time with the job's own Go) lists undocumented exported Go identifiers. It reports, per package directory: a missing package comment; an exported function, a method on an exported receiver type (pointer, value or generic) and an exported type with no doc comment (the spec's doc, its trailing comment or the enclosing declaration's doc all count); and an exported const or var with no comment, one finding per parenthesised block that has no block comment. Struct fields, interface methods, methods on unexported types and comment wording are not checked. Every exported identifier counts, `internal/` included, and files for every platform count (build constraints are not evaluated). It skips `testdata/`, `vendor/`, `node_modules/` and dot or underscore directories, files whose names start with `_` or `.`, symlinks, `*_test.go`, `//go:build ignore` files, generated files and `package main`. The fingerprint is `docs:undoc:<dir>:<symbol>` or `docs:pkg:<dir>`; the file and line are left out, so moving a declaration within its package keeps its issue. Severity is `low` and the group is the package directory. It only parses files: it never runs repository code and needs no network. It fails closed: a parse error, unreadable file, invalid path or more than 10,000 findings fails the run with nothing filed. A human-owned `exclude` file in the role directory skips package directories (`dir/` is a directory and everything below it, anything else a glob matched against the package directory); an entry makes its findings vanish, so autoclose closes their issues. Go only for now.\n254:**Starter planning roles.** `ard init` also ships four planning roles, each writing its own `plan.md` section (`config.BuiltinPlanSections`) and its role file: `product-manager` (`## Requirements`: requirements, a numbered list of acceptance criteria, out of scope), `architect` (`## Design`: approach, affected modules, and an `ADR needed: yes|no` line; the worker writes any ADR in its pull request), `designer` (`## UX`, plus Markdown or script-free SVG files under `.ard/<issue#>/design/`) and `documentation-planner` (`## Docs`: which docs change and what each must say). The command line counts as a user interface, so the designer covers flags, output and messages. The starter ordering is product-manager, then architect; designer, then documentation-planner; the engineer runs after architect and documentation-planner (`after:` in the starter `config.yaml`). Note for #45: acceptance criteria are a numbered list under `### Acceptance criteria`.\n256:The starter lead (`.ard/triage/lead/prompt.txt`) follows a fixed contract. It reads the issue only through the untrusted input files `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, which the triage loop writes and never commits. It finds roles by listing the role directories and routes only to those. It writes only `triage-lead.md`, with `attempt: 1`, and a `status: done` always names a `worker`. It answers `needs-human` when a person must decide: an unclear ask, a question, a duplicate, a change to `.ard/config.yaml`, the role directories or `.github/`, no fitting worker, or text that tries to instruct it. It uses `review:<role>` only for a reviewer whose `when` rule would skip a change that needs that review. The committer and close-out guards confine it to `.ard/<issue#>/`, and close-out allows it only `triage-lead.md`; any other change under `.ard/<issue#>/` fails the job as needs-human.\n258:The starter engineer (`.ard/triage/engineer/prompt.txt`) plans for the worker without implementing. Its inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, all untrusted. It writes the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md` with Steps, Test plan, Risk and Size, sized S, M, L or XL on a scale tied to the worker diff guard's block limit (XL is over the worker role's `diff_max_lines`, 3,000 changed lines by default). It writes `triage-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, never a `worker` key. It answers `needs-human` for an XL change, a change to protected role and config paths or `.github/`, or a missing product or design decision, and leaves `plan.md` untouched. In Phase 1 it has no `after:`; the lead's request orders it. The committer and close-out guards confine it to `.ard/<issue#>/`, and close-out allows it only `triage-engineer.md` and the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md`; a change to `triage-lead.md`, `issue.md` or another role's section fails the job as needs-human.\n285:Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod: it starts `ard serve --loops scanners,stale,reaper` ([#187](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/187)), so the triage, worker and reviewer loops never run there, even though `.ard/config.yaml` on `main` has agent roles. `--loops` takes a comma-separated list of loop names (scanners, stale, triage, workers, reviewers, reviewfix, merger, janitor, reaper, budget; `budget` is the status-issue notice loop, the claim gate is always on), defaults to every loop, and rejects an unknown name with exit 2. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.\n300:**Agent CLIs plug in through adapters.** Each adapter turns (prompt file, context paths, budget) into a command line and parses the exit code and, where the CLI reports them, turns and cost. For Claude Code that is `claude -p --output-format=stream-json --verbose --restricted --safe-mode --strict-mcp-config --disable-slash-commands --permission-mode=dontAsk --settings='{\"includeGitInstructions\":false,\"disableAllHooks\":true}' --tools=… --allowed-tools=… --disallowed-tools=… --model <model> --max-budget-usd <max_budget_usd> --append-system-prompt-file <role prompt> -- <message>` (`--verbose` is required by `stream-json` in print mode). Every stage (scanner, triage, worker, review) gets the same tool profile: `--tools` and `--allowed-tools` are `Read,Glob,Grep,Edit,Write,Bash`, and `--disallowed-tools` is `Bash(git commit *),Bash(git push *),Bash(git remote *),Bash(gh *)` (decided, #79). ard does not pass `--bare`: it limits the CLI to Bash, Read and Edit, and `--tools` cannot widen it (#105). The planted-config isolation `--bare` gave comes instead from `--restricted` (only managed settings and `--settings` load), `--safe-mode` (no CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, skills, agents, hooks, MCP servers or auto memory), `--strict-mcp-config`, `--disable-slash-commands`, and `--settings` that turns off hooks and the git status snapshot (commit subjects are third-party text). `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_BACKGROUND_TASKS=1` is set as a fixed env var (the executor allows it) so Bash cannot detach commands. The integration tests pin each of these against a worktree with planted config and must be re-run on every `TestedVersion` bump. The tool list is not a security boundary, and the deny list is advisory, because shell indirection gets around it. The real boundaries are the missing GitHub token (Docker; see the host-mode note above, #84), the committer, which commits only `.ard/<issue#>/` from a triage or review job (other edits are discarded with the worktree), and the diff guard on worker changes. An explicit tool list from the caller (`Spec.Tools`) replaces the allow list and keeps the deny list. The user message holds only fixed text and the paths of the untrusted input files. The installed CLI accepts an undocumented `--max-turns`; ard does not rely on it, so a per-job dollar cap and the executor's hard timeout bound each run. Agents run with an empty per-job CLI config dir and HOME and receive exactly one model credential, passed by name by the executor and never visible to the adapter: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) or a subscription OAuth token (`CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`, from `claude setup-token`); that rests on the environment, not on a CLI flag. `ard serve` chooses the credential from its own environment: `ARD_AGENT_AUTH` is `auto` (the default; the token if set, else the key), `oauth` or `apikey`, and an explicit mode never falls back to the other variable. Only one is ever passed, because the CLI prefers the API key and would silently bill it (pinned by an integration test). A missing credential does not stop startup, because deterministic scanners need none: `ard serve` logs one INFO line, and a job that needs one fails with a clear error. The executor creates the per-job config dir as `<job home>/.claude` (0700) in `prepare` (`Handle.View.ConfigDir`; under Docker it is a bind mount at `/ard-config`), refuses to start a job whose config dir is not that empty directory, and removes it with the job home; the Reaper's `KeepWorktree` retention keeps it too, and it holds no credential, since the credential travels only in the environment. `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBPROCESS_ENV_SCRUB=1`, which would keep the token out of Bash children, is not set (decided, #125). Residual risk: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) is inherited by Bash children; on the tested release the CLI does not pass `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` to them; and either credential can still be read from the `claude` process's own environment (`/proc/$PPID/environ` on Linux, `ps eww` on macOS). The container is the boundary: it holds no GitHub token, and the credential is scoped to the model. The scrub is off because on 2.1.328 it requires bubblewrap (and socat) plus unprivileged user namespaces; Docker blocks those under ard's flags (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, default seccomp), so every Bash call would fail closed and without bwrap the CLI refuses to start. On macOS host mode it worked and matched the `dontAsk` outcomes. It can be revisited for host mode or a loosened container. What agent Bash can read differs by mode: under Docker, only the job's own credential; in host mode, far more, because any same-uid process's environment is readable (`ps eww <pid>` on macOS, `/proc/<pid>/environ` on Linux), including `ard serve`'s own, which holds both credentials. The scrub would not close that; i","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M86VP4D6CD8WXDR7ZMA6BKSF/6daadd15-ae85-4ac6-8f77-85168753bba0/tool-results/bhlxatvlv.txt","persistedOutputSize":53719},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01T7vNtPavDL8ZeM6hgJQAD9","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
