{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DBtFoBAV2AoXgqx77uzUFZ","type":"tool_result","content":"5:95:## The .ard/ contract\n13:389:- the diff touches no protected path; the list comes only from `merge.protected_paths`, with no floor in code (decided, #3), so a human who empties the list removes the gate. `ard init` proposes the defaults `.github/`, `.ard/config.yaml`, the four role trees (`.ard/scanners/`, `.ard/triage/`, `.ard/workers/`, `.ard/reviewers/`), `**/CODEOWNERS`, and the agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents: `CLAUDE.md`, `**/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `**/AGENTS.md`, `.claude/` and `.mcp.json`, and the linter config a PR could weaken, `**/.golangci.yml`, `**/.golangci.yaml`, `**/.golangci.toml` and `**/.golangci.json` (17 entries; `**/X` already matches a root `X`, the bare entries are kept for clarity). An entry ending in `/` is a directory prefix; any other entry is a doublestar glob. Matching is case-insensitive on the repo-relative path (macOS file systems are, so `Claude.md` and `.Claude/x` are protected). A prefix matches on a directory boundary (`.github/` matches `.github` and `.github/x`, never `.githubx`). A glob follows doublestar: `*` stays within one path segment and `**` crosses them, so a bare `CODEOWNERS` would match only the root file. Quote globs that start with `*` in YAML. The merger checks both names of a renamed file; a malformed path counts as protected. A PR that touches one gets `ard:needs-approval` and merges on the next poll once a person with write access approves the current head with a GitHub review (below).\n15:413:## Configuration\n16:478:## Guardrails\n17:489:- **Role files are human-owned.** The diff guard and the committer's code lane reject any worker change under `.ard/` or `.github/` (so `.ard/config.yaml` and every role directory), and the merger treats those paths as protected through `merge.protected_paths` (the `ard init` defaults), so no agent can rewrite its